# Polling Queue Implementation

## Problem Description
Implement a thread-safe `PollingQueue<T>` class that manages a queue of elements with controlled polling behavior. The queue should:
1. Allow elements to be added (enqueued) and removed (polled)
2. Track and limit the number of polling attempts
3. Provide waiting functionality for when the queue is empty
4. Support resetting the polling attempt counter
5. Implement proper equality comparison and hash code generation

## Class Requirements
You must implement the `PollingQueue<T>` class with the following exact specifications:

```java
class PollingQueue<T> {
    private final Queue<T> queue;
    private final AtomicInteger pollingCount;
    private final int maxPollingAttempts;

    public PollingQueue(int maxPollingAttempts) {
        // Initialize the queue, polling counter, and store max attempts
    }

    public synchronized void enqueue(T element) {
        // Add element to queue and notify waiting threads
    }

    public synchronized T poll() {
        // Return next element or throw if max polls exceeded
    }

    public synchronized T waitForElement(long timeoutMillis) throws InterruptedException {
        // Wait for element with timeout, return null if timeout occurs
    }

    public void resetPollingCount() {
        // Reset the polling attempt counter
    }

    public int getPollingCount() {
        // Return current polling count
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        // Implement equality comparison
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        // Implement hash code generation
    }
}
```

## Constraints
1. The queue must be thread-safe for all operations
2. Maximum polling attempts must be enforced
3. Waiting operations should properly handle timeouts
4. Equality comparison should consider all relevant fields
5. Hash code generation should follow standard practices

## Example Usage

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create queue with max 3 polling attempts
        PollingQueue<String> queue = new PollingQueue<>(3);

        // Enqueue some items
        queue.enqueue("First");
        queue.enqueue("Second");

        // Poll items
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Prints "First"
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Prints "Second"

        // Try polling empty queue
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Returns null
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Returns null
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Throws IllegalStateException

        // Reset counter and try again
        queue.resetPollingCount();
        System.out.println(queue.poll()); // Returns null

        // Wait for element with timeout
        new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(1000);
                queue.enqueue("Delayed");
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }).start();

        try {
            System.out.println(queue.waitForElement(2000)); // Prints "Delayed"
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

## Notes
1. Your implementation must exactly match the method signatures and field declarations shown
2. Pay special attention to thread safety in all synchronized methods
3. The `waitForElement` method should properly handle the timeout case
4. Equality comparison should consider both the queue contents and polling state
5. Do not modify the given class structure or method signatures

