# Web Fragment Simulator Implementation

## Problem Description
You are tasked with implementing a `WebFragmentSimulator` class that models the behavior of a web fragment in a mobile application. This simulator should handle web content loading under different conditions and manage activity transitions with intent extras.

The class should:
1. Simulate loading web content with various status conditions (success, error, pending)
2. Handle URL modifications for specific cases
3. Manage intent extras for activity transitions
4. Track the current state of the fragment (visibility, network availability)

## Class Requirements
Implement the `WebFragmentSimulator` class with the following exact specifications:

```java
class WebFragmentSimulator {
    private String currentUrl;
    private boolean isFragmentVisible;
    private Map<String, String> intentExtras;
    private boolean networkAvailable;

    public WebFragmentSimulator() {
        // Initialize fields
    }

    public Map<String, Object> loadWebContent(String url, boolean isFragmentVisible, boolean networkAvailable) {
        // Implement according to specifications
    }

    private String appendUrlParameters(String originalUrl) {
        // Implement according to specifications
    }

    public void startNewActivity(int targetActivity, String title, String url, boolean isExternal) {
        // Implement according to specifications
    }

    public Map<String, String> getIntentExtras() {
        // Implement according to specifications
    }
}
```

## Method Specifications

### `loadWebContent(String url, boolean isFragmentVisible, boolean networkAvailable)`
- Returns a Map containing the load status and additional information
- Behavior:
  - If fragment is not visible: return status "pending" with message "Fragment not visible - loading deferred"
  - If network is unavailable: return status "error" with message "Network unavailable"
  - If URL is null or empty: return status "error" with message "Invalid URL"
  - On success: return status "success" with the URL and current timestamp
  - For URLs containing "ziroomupin": include a modified URL with appended parameters

### `appendUrlParameters(String originalUrl)`
- Private helper method
- Appends "app_version=1.0&os=android" to the URL
- Handles both URLs with and without existing parameters

### `startNewActivity(int targetActivity, String title, String url, boolean isExternal)`
- Prepares intent extras for a new activity
- Stores title, URL, and activity type
- For external URLs, modifies the URL with appended parameters

### `getIntentExtras()`
- Returns a copy of the current intent extras map

## Example Usage

```java
WebFragmentSimulator simulator = new WebFragmentSimulator();

// Example 1: Successful load
Map<String, Object> result = simulator.loadWebContent("https://example.com", true, true);
System.out.println(result.get("status"));  // Prints: "success"
System.out.println(result.get("url"));     // Prints: "https://example.com"

// Example 2: Activity transition
simulator.startNewActivity(3, "Settings", "https://settings.com", true);
Map<String, String> extras = simulator.getIntentExtras();
System.out.println(extras.get("title"));   // Prints: "Settings"
System.out.println(extras.get("url"));     // Prints modified URL
```

## Constraints
- All method signatures must match exactly as specified
- Field declarations must not be modified
- The solution must be implemented in Java
- Do not add any additional public methods

## Evaluation Criteria
Your implementation will be tested against various scenarios including:
- Different URL loading conditions (success, error, pending cases)
- URL parameter appending logic
- Activity intent extra management
- State maintenance between method calls

Note: The test cases will verify all edge cases and functionality, but are not provided here to avoid solution hints.

