        // Creates a linked list from a list of integers
    }

    public static List<Integer> linkedListToList(LinkedListNode<Integer> head) {
        // Converts a linked list back to a list of integers
    }
}
```

## Method Specifications

### `swapNodes` method
- **Parameters**: 
  - `head`: The head node of the linked list
  - `i`: First position to swap (0-based index)
  - `j`: Second position to swap (0-based index)
- **Returns**: The head of the modified linked list after swapping
- **Behavior**:
  - If either index is invalid (out of bounds), return the original list unchanged
  - If i == j, return the original list unchanged
  - Handle special cases for adjacent nodes and head/tail swaps
  - The method should work for any valid pair of indices in the list

### Helper Methods
- `createLinkedList`: Creates a linked list from a List of integers
- `linkedListToList`: Converts a linked list back to a List of integers

## Constraints
- The linked list may contain up to 1000 nodes
- Node values will be integers between -1000 and 1000
- Indices i and j will be non-negative integers
- The original order of nodes (except the swapped ones) must be preserved

## Example Usage

### Example 1: Basic Swap
```java
List<Integer> elements = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
LinkedListNode<Integer> head = LinkedListSwapper.createLinkedList(elements);
LinkedListNode<Integer> swapped = LinkedListSwapper.swapNodes(head, 1, 3);
List<Integer> result = LinkedListSwapper.linkedListToList(swapped);
// result will be [1, 4, 3, 2, 5]
