# Enhanced MergeSort Implementation

## Problem Description

Implement an enhanced version of the MergeSort algorithm that supports:
1. Sorting in both ascending and descending order
2. Working with generic types (not limited to specific data types)
3. Proper handling of edge cases (null input, empty arrays, single-element arrays)
4. Custom comparators for specialized sorting logic

Your implementation should exactly match the class structure and method signatures provided below.

## Class Requirements

You must implement the following class with all specified methods:

```java
class EnhancedMergeSort {

    /**
     * Main sorting method that implements MergeSort
     * @param <T> The type of elements in the array
     * @param array The array to be sorted
     * @param comparator The comparator to determine the order of elements
     * @param ascending If true, sorts in ascending order; otherwise descending
     * @return The sorted array
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if input array is null
     */
    public <T> T[] sort(T[] array, Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        // Your implementation
    }

    /**
     * Merges two sorted arrays into one sorted array
     * @param left The left sorted array
     * @param right The right sorted array
     * @param array The target array to store merged result
     * @param comparator The comparator to determine element order
     * @param ascending The sorting direction
     * @return The merged and sorted array
     */
    private <T> T[] merge(T[] left, T[] right, T[] array, 
                         Comparator<? super T> comparator, boolean ascending) {
        // Your implementation
    }

    /**
     * Helper method to sort integers in ascending order
     * @param array The integer array to sort
     * @return The sorted array
     */
    public Integer[] sortIntegers(Integer[] array) {
        // Your implementation
    }

    /**
     * Helper method to sort strings in ascending order
     * @param array The string array to sort
     * @return The sorted array
     */
    public String[] sortStrings(String[] array) {
        // Your implementation
    }
}
```

## Constraints

1. The implementation must be purely in Java
2. You may not add or remove any methods or fields from the class
3. All method signatures must remain exactly as specified
4. The implementation must handle null inputs by throwing IllegalArgumentException
5. The sort must be stable (equal elements maintain their relative order)
6. The implementation must work for any generic type T

## Example Usage

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EnhancedMergeSort sorter = new EnhancedMergeSort();

        // Example 1: Sorting integers in ascending order
        Integer[] numbers = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
        Integer[] sortedNumbers = sorter.sortIntegers(numbers);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sortedNumbers));
        // Output: [1, 2, 5, 5, 6, 9]

        // Example 2: Sorting strings with custom comparator (by length)
        String[] fruits = {"apple", "banana", "grape", "kiwi"};
        String[] sortedFruits = sorter.sort(fruits, Comparator.comparingInt(String::length), true);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sortedFruits));
        // Output: [kiwi, apple, grape, banana]

        // Example 3: Sorting in descending order
        Integer[] nums = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9};
        Integer[] sortedDesc = sorter.sort(nums, Comparator.naturalOrder(), false);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(sortedDesc));
        // Output: [9, 5, 4, 3, 1, 1]
    }
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria

Your implementation will be tested against various cases including:
1. Standard sorting cases (ascending and descending)
2. Edge cases (empty array, single element array)
3. Custom comparator scenarios
4. Null input handling
5. Stability of the sort (for equal elements)
6. Performance on larger arrays

Note: Do not modify the method signatures or add any additional public methods. The private merge method must remain private.

