task_details.source_specification:
# Binary Search Tree Implementation

## Problem Description
Implement a generic binary search tree class called `EnhancedBinarySearchTree` that stores elements in a sorted order and provides efficient operations for adding, removing, and searching elements. The tree should maintain the binary search tree property where for each node:
- All elements in the left subtree are less than the node's element
- All elements in the right subtree are greater than the node's element

## Class Requirements
Your implementation must include exactly these components:

```java
class EnhancedBinarySearchTree<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private Node root;
    private int size;

    private class Node {
        T data;
        Node left, right;

        Node(T data) {
            this.data = data;
        }
    }

    public boolean add(T element) { /* implementation */ }
    private boolean add(Node node, T element) { /* implementation */ }

    public T first() { /* implementation */ }
    public T last() { /* implementation */ }

    public boolean contains(T element) { /* implementation */ }
    private boolean contains(Node node, T element) { /* implementation */ }

    public boolean remove(T element) { /* implementation */ }
    private Node remove(Node node, T element) { /* implementation */ }
    private Node findMin(Node node) { /* implementation */ }

    public boolean isEmpty() { /* implementation */ }
    public int size() { /* implementation */ }
    public void clear() { /* implementation */ }
}
```

## Method Specifications

### Public Methods
1. `boolean add(T element)`
   - Adds the specified element to the tree if it is not already present
   - Returns true if the element was added, false if it was already present
   - Throws NullPointerException if the element is null

2. `T first()`
   - Returns the smallest element in the tree
   - Throws NoSuchElementException if the tree is empty

3. `T last()`
   - Returns the largest element in the tree
   - Throws NoSuchElementException if the tree is empty

4. `boolean contains(T element)`
   - Returns true if the tree contains the specified element, false otherwise

5. `boolean remove(T element)`
   - Removes the specified element from the tree if it is present
   - Returns true if the element was removed, false if it wasn't found
   - Throws NullPointerException if the element is null

6. `boolean isEmpty()`
   - Returns true if the tree contains no elements

7. `int size()`
   - Returns the number of elements in the tree

8. `void clear()`
   - Removes all elements from the tree

## Constraints
- The tree must maintain the binary search tree property at all times
- All operations must maintain O(h) time complexity where h is the height of the tree
- Null elements are not allowed in the tree
- The implementation must be generic and work with any Comparable type

## Example Usage

```java
EnhancedBinarySearchTree<Integer> tree = new EnhancedBinarySearchTree<>();

// Adding elements
tree.add(50);
tree.add(30);
tree.add(70);
tree.add(20);
tree.add(40);
tree.add(60);
tree.add(80);

// Checking size and emptiness
System.out.println("Size: " + tree.size()); // Output: 7
System.out.println("Is empty: " + tree.isEmpty()); // Output: false

// Getting first and last elements
System.out.println("First element: " + tree.first()); // Output: 20
System.out.println("Last element: " + tree.last()); // Output: 80

// Checking contains
System.out.println("Contains 40: " + tree.contains(40)); // Output: true
System.out.println("Contains 45: " + tree.contains(45)); // Output: false

// Removing elements
System.out.println("Remove 20: " + tree.remove(20)); // Output: true
System.out.println("Remove 45: " + tree.remove(45)); // Output: false

// Clearing the tree
tree.clear();
System.out.println("Size after clear: " + tree.size()); // Output: 0
```

## Notes
- You must implement all methods exactly as specified
- Do not use any Java Collection Framework classes in your implementation
- The tree should automatically maintain its sorted property
- Pay special attention to edge cases (empty tree, removing nodes with different numbers of children)

